# Abstracts

## Luya Wang [](<$section.id("Luya Wang")>)

### Title: Interactions between smooth and symplectic topology

># [**Abstract**]($block.collapsible(false))
> Symplectic structures roughly lie between smooth topology and complex geometry
> and exhibit both flexible and rigid phenomena.
> However, we still do not have a good understanding of what a general symplectic manifold should look like.
>
> In this talk, I will begin by surveying some classical results of Gromov and McDuff from the 1980s,
> which use pseudoholomorphic curves to classify symplectic manifolds containing certain distinguished submanifolds.
>
> I will then discuss a question of Donaldson in the 1990s,
> which relates smooth structures in dimension four to symplectic structures in dimension six.
>
> Finally, I will describe some recent refinements of the Gromov-McDuff techniques
> and their applications to classification problems in the compact setting,
> which has many interactions with low-dimensional topology.

## Michael Dougherty [](<$section.id("Michael Dougherty")>)

### Title: Geometric Combinatorics for Complex Polynomials
># [**Abstract**]($block.collapsible(false))
> Spaces of complex polynomials contain a surprising amount of combinatorial structure,
> particularly in connections to noncrossing partitions.
> In this talk we will discuss how to extract this noncrossing data from a polynomial
> and how it determines a compactification and cell structure for a space of polynomials.
> This is joint work with Jon McCammond.

## Spencer Leslie [](<$section.id("Spencer Leslie")>)

### Title: L-functions and periods
># [**Abstract**]($block.collapsible(false))
> Zeta and L-functions play a central role in modern number theory.
> In this talk, I will discuss some classical contexts where these functions and their properties arise
> and how we can study them via a different kind of analytic object: a modular or automorphic form.
> The process of relating a modular form to an L-function is by forming a "period integral".
> My goal is to give a sense (through examples) of how the study of periods has taken a central place in modern number theory.
> I will give an overview of some recent techniques and results in this area of research.

## Jeremy Brazas [](<$section.id("Jeremy Brazas")>)

### Title: The infinite earring group and its applications
># [**Abstract**]($block.collapsible(true))
> The infinite earring group, i.e. the fundamental group of a shrinking wedge of circles,
> is one of the first examples of a fundamental group
> that cannot be computed using standard methods in algebraic topology.
> This group is uncountable, not free, and perhaps best thought of as an infinitary analogue of a free group.
> In this talk, I’ll give a friendly introduction to the methods used to characterize this group and derive its properties.
> I’ll also discuss how it has been applied in recent decades and mention a few open questions.

## Hannah Hoganson [](<$section.id("Hannah Hoganson")>)

### Title: A Tale of Three Geometries: Points, Hamming Cubes, and One-Ended Trees
># [**Abstract**]($block.collapsible(true))
> Many groups arise naturally as the symmetry group of a geometric or topological object.
> In this talk we will focus on infinite but 0-dimensional topological spaces,
> specifically those that arise as end spaces.
> Their homeomorphism groups can be thought of as infinite analogs of symmetric groups.
> We will start with an overview of some basic ideas in geometric group theory,
> before focusing in on the homeomorphism groups of countable end spaces.
> These are large topological groups, which are not finitely generated,
> so classical tools and techniques don't apply.
> Instead, we turn to descriptive set theoretic techniques of Rosendal
> to study their large-scale geometry and present a trichotomy classification theorem.
> This talk includes joint work with George Domat and Robbie Lyman.
